Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers to industrial field devices
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns response objects with .isError()/.registers; pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk return raw lists or None on failure, requiring 
    │   different error-checking logic; minimalmodbus lacks a 'unit' keyword in the same form and doesn't return response objects with isError(); it raises 
    │   exceptions or returns raw values directly; Connection lifecycle differs: some libs require open()/close() instead of connect(), or auto-manage 
    │   connections per call; Address/function-code handling (e.g., holding vs input registers) may differ between libraries; Concurrency/threading safety 
    │   varies across these clients
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All are Python Modbus master/client libraries capable of TCP communication with PLCs/RTUs for reading and writing holding registers, matching the 
        file's core need (read_holding_registers/write_register with unit id). pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk expose comparable client objects with 
        connect/read/write register methods usable as drop-in replacements with moderate glue. minimalmodbus is primarily serial/RTU-oriented with a 
        fundamentally different synchronous per-call API (no persistent client object, no isError() result wrapper, uses read_register/write_register directly 
        returning values or raising exceptions) so it requires substantial rewrite of error handling and I/O flow.

